# Day 13 - Advanced Selenium & Automation Frameworks

## Objective
To learn advanced Selenium concepts, handle dynamic web elements, implement waits, build a Page Object Model (POM) framework,
and organize automation tests using TestNG.

---

## Tools Used
- Java JDK 21
- Eclipse IDE
- Selenium WebDriver
- TestNG
- Maven
- Google Chrome
- ChromeDriver

---

## Topics Covered

### 1. Selenium Waits
Learned different synchronization techniques:
- Implicit Wait
- Explicit Wait
- Fluent Wait

Also understood:
- When to use each wait
- Why `Thread.sleep()` should be avoided

---

### 2. Handling Complex Scenarios
Practiced handling:
- JavaScript Alerts
- Frames and iFrames
- Multiple Windows and Tabs
- Mouse Hover (Actions Class)
- Drag and Drop
- Right Click
- Double Click
- Keyboard Actions

---

### 3. Handling Dynamic Elements
Learned techniques for handling:
- Dynamic IDs
- AJAX-loaded elements
- Loading spinners
- Elements that appear and disappear dynamically

---

### 4. Page Object Model (POM)
Learned the Page Object Model design pattern:
- Separate Page Classes
- Store Locators inside Page Classes
- Create Action Methods
- Improve code reusability and maintenance

Implemented:
- LoginPage
- HomePage
- BaseTest

---

### 5. TestNG Framework
Practiced TestNG features:
- `@Test`
- `@BeforeMethod`
- `@AfterMethod`
- Assertions
- Test Suites (`testng.xml`)
- Test Priorities
- Data Providers
- Parallel Execution (Basics)

---

### 6. Automation Framework Structure
Created a basic automation framework with:
- Base Test Class
- Page Classes
- Test Classes
- Utility Classes
- Configuration Files
- Test Data Files

---

## Deliverables Completed
- Advanced Selenium scripts created
- Selenium waits implemented
- Page Object Model (POM) implemented
- TestNG test suite created
- Basic automation framework developed
- README with framework documentation completed

---

## Conclusion
Day 13 focused on advanced Selenium automation concepts, including synchronization techniques, handling dynamic web elements,
implementing the Page Object Model, and building a structured automation framework using Selenium, Java, and TestNG.
